/target
**/*.rs.bk
Cargo.lock.bak

# Generated test material. Nothing in here is committed, not even the public
# halves: it is throwaway and reproducible with tools/gen-testcert.sh. In
# particular *.pk8.der and *.p12 are private keys.
/testdata/certs/*
!/testdata/certs/README.md
# the policy OIDs signer-policy declares are plain text, and a unit test reads
# them to know what the signingCertificate policies field must carry. The file
# is derived from tools/gen-testcert.sh itself, never private.
!/testdata/certs/signer-policy.policies.txt
/testdata/golden/*
!/testdata/golden/README.md

# Certificate, key and signature material, anywhere in the tree. A signature
# embeds the signer certificate and its whole chain, so a file signed with a
# real FNMT or DNIe certificate carries the holder's name and DNI number.
# tools/check-no-real-certs.sh is the authority and runs on pre-commit and in
# CI, this list only keeps git status quiet.
*.p12
*.pfx
*.p8
*.p7b
*.p7s
*.p10
*.p1m
*.csr
*.key
*.pem
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
*.jks
*.keystore
*.csig
*.xsig
*.sig
cert9.db
key4.db
cert8.db
secmod.db
cacerts
autofirma.pfx

# output signed by hand while testing
*.signed.pdf
/tmp/

# the generated third-party licence report (tools/ABOUT_TEMPLATE.hbs + `cargo
# about generate` produce it in the release job from this lockfile); a copy
# committed here would drift from the lockfile it is supposed to describe.
/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html

# Stray compiler output (never belongs in git): a 400 KiB ELF that no
guard or test needs and that every whole-file scan has to chew through.
rust_out
